{"name":"Reef Gold","hex":"#a98d36","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"reef","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chain or range of rocks, sand, or coral lying at or near the surface of the water.","A large vein of auriferous quartz; hence, any body of rock yielding valuable ore."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A portion of a sail rolled and tied down to lessen the area exposed in a high wind.","A reef knot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/reef"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Quartz reef mining","blocks":[{"text":"Quartz reef mining is a type of gold mining in \"reefs\" (veins) of quartz.\nQuartz is one of the most common minerals in the Earth's crust, and most quartz veins do not carry gold, but those that have gold are avidly hunted by prospectors.\nIn the shallow, oxidized zones of quartz reef deposits, the gold occurs in its metallic state, and is easily recovered with simple equipment.\nQuartz reef mining played an important role in 19th century gold-mining districts such as Bendigo, Victoria in Australia, Central Otago in New Zealand, and the California mother lode."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Paynes_Find_030923_Gnangarra-13.jpg/330px-Paynes_Find_030923_Gnangarra-13.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quartz_reef_mining","wikidata":"Q7269402"},{"title":"Reef","blocks":[{"text":"A reef is a ridge or shoal of rock, coral, or similar relatively stable material lying beneath the surface of a natural body of water. Many reefs result from natural, abiotic (non-living) processes such as deposition of sand or wave erosion planing down rock outcrops. However, reefs such as the coral reefs of tropical waters are formed by biotic (living) processes, dominated by corals and coralline algae. Artificial reefs, such as shipwrecks and other man-made underwater structures, may occur intentionally or as the result of an accident. These are sometimes designed to increase the physical complexity of featureless sand bottoms to attract a more diverse range of organisms. They provide shelter to various aquatic animals which help prevent extinction. Another reason reefs are put in place is for aquaculture, and fish farmers who are looking to improve their businesses sometimes invest in them. Reefs are often quite near to the surface, but not all definitions require this."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Nusa_Lembongan_Reef.jpg/330px-Nusa_Lembongan_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reef","wikidata":"Q184358"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Kilimafeza001.jpg/500px-Kilimafeza001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Kilimafeza001.jpg/1280px-Kilimafeza001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Kilimafeza001.jpg/500px-Kilimafeza001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Headframe with gin wheel. Abandoned Kilimafeza gold mine, Serengeti NP, Tanzania","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKilimafeza001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/PamalicanAfterLiftOff.jpg/500px-PamalicanAfterLiftOff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/PamalicanAfterLiftOff.jpg/1280px-PamalicanAfterLiftOff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/PamalicanAfterLiftOff.jpg/500px-PamalicanAfterLiftOff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pamalican island with surrounding reef, Sulu Sea, Philippines","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APamalicanAfterLiftOff.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Reef.jpg/500px-Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Reef.jpg/1280px-Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Reef.jpg/500px-Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_c